\DOC LOCAL_NSQR_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_NSQR_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-execution goal across one inlined
repeated-squaring subroutine call, replacing the current state variable by its
successor and rewriting the state assumptions accordingly.

\DESCRIBE
The single argument is an {int}, the index of the new state: the state variable
in the resulting goal and assumptions carries this index, which is one more
than the index in the incoming goal. The conclusion retains its shape — an
{eventually} assertion about a postcondition and a frame — with the final state
argument replaced by the new numbered state. Every assumption mentioning the
old state is discharged and replaced by a corresponding assumption about the
new state: the program-code assumption, the register and memory-content
assumptions carried through the call, a new assumption giving the result
written at the output buffer as a modular repeated-squaring expression in the
input value, an updated instruction-pointer assumption, and an updated
accumulated frame ({MAYCHANGE ... s0 sNew}), which may gain extra components
for the registers, flags and memory ranges clobbered by the call. Assumptions
that do not refer to the state (side conditions such as non-overlapping and
arithmetic facts) are left untouched, and exactly one subgoal results.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals of this {eventually} form with a full set of machine-state assumptions.

\ENDDOC
